**Definition 2.1 (the one enlarged native runtime).** Let $P$ be exactly
RCA Definitions 11.1–14.2's late-installed machine. Before activation it
executes the original AIF source dispatcher. At an original post-acquisition
Ready, the actual controller owner with the prescribed asset authorities
may choose Install once. This executes the single bounded bootstrap-entry
CTL and receipt of RCA Definition 14.1. Install is disabled at every old
busy PC. The ordinary Ready continuation remains a separate possible action.

Installation really executes the first fresh bitwise bootstrap with $E(L)$,
the capture controller's ingress of $101\,U(|\Delta(D_1)|)\Delta(D_1)$,
the second bootstrap with $E(D_1)$, actual authorized asset copies, manifest
construction, native empty-bank construction, full-prefix import and final
receiver/service-token handoffs. Only DONE enables a completed new table.
The fixed finite NEXT/INPUT/WRITE/ADVANCE/DONE mechanism and its cursor have
their actual native meanings. Old control bits remain retained and inactive.
Canonical supply requires precisely those three public code words and the
declared equality of installed $E(D_1)$ with the serialization of the
captured $\Delta(D_1)$. An archival description and its native boot encoding
are distinct words. No self-description fixed point is used.

After installation every external source or service frame is actually
retained on port 38. Tags 000–100 invoke the original source handlers by
RCA's two-row RD/MR replacement for each old public IN. Tags 101, 110 and
111 supply Cover, Next/Select and Call with precisely RCA's payload syntax.
The source-path clone reprocesses the acquired frame on the original
ingress ports; those RD(38) occurrences are not new external input. The
fixed graph has no independent waiting, printing or scheduling choice.

